Body:
Fix for "Phantom" / Double Prompts in OpenCode + LM Studio
The Issue:
When you send your first message in a session, OpenCode sends a hidden background request to the LLM with a system prompt like You are a title generator. On local models (especially large ones like Qwen 35B), this "phantom" request consumes GPU resources, eats up tokens, and delays the actual response to your query.
The Solution:
You need to explicitly disable the internal title agent in your OpenCode configuration.
Open your global config file (usually located at ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json).
Add the "agent" block with the "title": { "disable": true } parameter to the root of the JSON:


{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai",
  "agent": {
    "title": {
      "disable": true
    }
  },
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio (local)",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0"
      }
    }
  }
}


Optional: If phantom requests persist (for summarization or other tasks), add "smallModel": null to the root of the config.
Restart your OpenCode session.
This prevents the CLI from trying to automatically name the thread, which stops the extra load on your local LM Studio server.

@r9614204368-netizen I was able to get it to work by making an opencode.json file where I open opencode from (I also set `OPENCODE_EXPERIMENTAL_LSP_TOOL=true`).
```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "permission": {
    "lsp": "allow"
  },
  "lsp": {
    "clangd": {
      "command": [
        "clangd",
        "--compile-commands-dir=<your build directory with the compile_commands.json file>"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

For ROS c++ projects, the build directory is not in the src directory. The folder structure is
```
ros_ws/
 |- build/compile_commands.json
 |- src/package/CMakeLists.txt
```

If I'm not mistaken, `NearestRoot` is excluding parent folders, so even if the compile_commands.json file was in `ros_ws/compile_commands.json`, it wouldn't find it. It would be nice if we could configure the build directory explicitly. It would also be helpful to add a note to https://opencode.ai/docs/lsp#how-it-works which talks about the location of compile commands, or how to configure the build directory for clangd.

I believe this is how we do it now, but making this explicit in the documentation would help.
```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "permission": {
    "lsp": "allow"
  },
  "lsp": {
    "clangd": {
      "command": [
        "clangd",
        "--compile-commands-dir=<your build directory with the compile_commands.json file>"
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

